go 1.26.10

// Six modules live in this repo. authn is separate for the opposite of a
// historical reason: it is what production services import to validate
// tokens (ADR 006, #211), and a module of its own is what keeps chameleon,
// connect-go, the proxy and the registry out of their binaries — its go.mod
// requires a JOSE library and nothing else, and authn/deps_test.go enforces
// that the arrow never turns around. client is separate for the same reason
// one step earlier (#36): it is what every fleet service imports to join
// local development, and its module boundary is what keeps the root module —
// and chameleon behind it — off their build graphs;
// client/deps_test.go holds that arrow. proto is separate because it is the
// leaf those two both stand on: the daemon serves the generated schemas and
// client speaks them, so the bindings can be required by each without either
// requiring the other. test/trailer is separate because it predates
// the root module, and stays separate to keep grpc-go — the whole point of
// that harness — out of what voya builds and links: no package voya builds
// imports it, and nothing it ships links it.
//
// Through chameleon v0.22.1 the claim had to be narrower, because the root
// module got grpc-go anyway: internal/config -> chameleon/resolver ->
// chameleon/sops -> sops/v4/keyservice, 159 packages linked into cmd/voya.
// The split bought only what the module *declared*. chameleon v0.23.0 ended
// that (#28) — `go mod why -m google.golang.org/grpc` now answers "main
// module does not need module google.golang.org/grpc" — so the split is once
// again about the dependency itself and not merely about its label.
//
// #225 narrowed it a third time, and #236 restored it rather than rewording
// it: test/acceptance's REST fixture requires connectrpc.com/vanguard, and
// while that fixture was a package of the root module, vanguard's own tests
// dragged grpc-go back into this module's build list — `GOWORK=off go list
// -m google.golang.org/grpc` answered v1.79.3 where before it answered
// nothing. Decided 2026-08-20: test/acceptance is a module of its own, for
// the reason test/trailer is, and the root module's manifest is vanguard-free
// again — that same command answers "not a known dependency", and `go mod
// why -m` answers "main module does not need module google.golang.org/grpc"
// as the paragraph above says. The alternative, narrowing the claim to
// "nothing voya *builds* links a transcoder", was rejected because amending
// a record to match what happened is how a repository stops being able to
// trust its own documents; cmd/voya/deps_test.go keeps the binary-level pin
// regardless. test/acceptance imports the root module — the opposite arrow
// from client and authn, and correct for a suite that starts the daemon — and
// carries intra-repo replaces for the root and all three siblings, so that
// GOWORK=off it exercises the checkout and not the published tags; its go.mod
// makes both arguments.
//
// test/trailer now proxies through voya itself (issue #21), which the
// workspace is what makes work here. Its go.mod also carries an intra-repo
// `replace` for the GOWORK=off case; the reasoning is written out there.
//
// `go test ./...` does NOT span modules — the Makefile and CI run each one
// explicitly. See `make test`.
//
// For cross-repo work against local chameleon / protowire-go checkouts, do not
// `go work use` them here: that edit is easy to commit by accident and breaks
// every build that does not have the sibling on disk. Copy this file to the
// git-ignored go.local.work, add the sibling there, and select it per-shell:
//
//	cp go.work go.local.work
//	GOWORK=$PWD/go.local.work go work use ../chameleon
//	GOWORK=$PWD/go.local.work go test ./...
//
// ADR 001 rejects `replace` directives in go.mod for those siblings, for the
// same reason. The intra-repo one mentioned above is a different case, and
// test/trailer/go.mod is where that argument is made.
use (
	.
	./authn
	./client
	./examples
	./proto
	./test/acceptance
	./test/trailer
)
